                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "id": "nKnHP",
                          "name": "Phone 390 wide · idle · t = 2 s caption",
                          "fill": "#5A5240",
                          "content": "Phone 390 wide · idle · t = 2 s",
                          "fontFamily": "Schibsted Grotesk",
                          "fontSize": 14,
                          "fontWeight": "normal"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "H5i8V",
                      "name": "Notes",
                      "fill": "#1A1400",
                      "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                      "width": 640,
                      "content": "Four globes made of about 160,000 dots. Each dot sits on a sphere, is turned and projected with plain 3D maths, and its brightness is the globe's formula times how much it faces the light, so the globes read as solid and lit. Formulas: noise-bent bands, three crossing sine waves (a hex lattice), a 4x4 ordered dither on the rim, and a ringed globe whose ring dots orbit faster near the middle.\n\nMotion: one turn every 40 to 90 s; globes drift and pass in front of each other over minutes. Checking: spin 60% faster, every globe pulses in step every 1.3 s. Refused: globes drift away from the text and out of frame. Success: lit sides resolve to #C6F135 and the big globe swells forward over 2 s, then holds.\n\nPalette: space #0A0E1C, haze #1A1E3A, text #E8ECF4. Font: Berkeley Mono. Cost: 13 ms per frame at half resolution. Next: globes casting shadows on each other.",
                      "lineHeight": 1.5,
                      "fontFamily": "Schibsted Grotesk",
                      "fontSize": 18,
                      "fontWeight": "normal"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
